HOW JAVA EVOLVED:30 YEARS OF STEADY UPGRADE
• Java 1.0 (Jan 1996): Introduced the Java Virtual Machine (JVM), core libraries, and applets under the "Write Once, Run Anywhere" philosophy.
• Java 5 (Sep 2004)  : Added major language features including generics, annotations, and enums. (Note: Partially obscured by the person in the foreground).
• Java 8 (Mar 2014)  : Brought functional programming capabilities with lambdas, the Stream API, and a new date/time API.
• Java 11 (Sep 2018) : Marked the first LTS release under the new 6-month release cadence and introduced a standard HTTP Client.
• Java 17 (Sep 2021) : Delivered modern language features like records, sealed classes, and text blocks, serving as the baseline for Spring Boot 3 and 4.
• Java 21 (Sep 2023) : Introduced virtual threads (Project Loom), pattern matching for switch statements, and record patterns.
• Java 25 (Sep 2025) : Highlighted features include compact source files, scoped values, and compact object headers.


